            <ArticleTitle>Comparison of efficacy of oral rofecoxib and ketorolac in controlling early postoperative outpatient orthopedic surgical pain.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>510-3</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We compared the efficacy of rofecoxib and ketorolac in controlling postoperative pain after outpatient surgery. Patients were randomly assigned to receive either rofecoxib or ketorolac preoperatively and every morning for 5 postoperative days. Patients in both groups received oxycodone as needed for breakthrough pain. After 5 postoperative days, patients were asked for information-pain score; number of supplemental analgesics used; and severity of any incision-site bleeding, nausea, or diarrhea. Rofecoxib and ketorolac did not differ on these measures. Rofecoxib and ketorolac are equally effective in controlling postoperative pain.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
